course: dictionary
lesson: 7836
title: Race: a contest and a fast run
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A race is a contest to see who is fastest. To race means to run or go very fast, often against other people. Look at the words around the word to know which meaning it has.
explain: «Theo lost three races in a row and threw the controller onto the sofa.» The word races is a noun here, because Theo lost three contests. The clue is that he lost, so there were winners and losers. You also know it was a contest because he lost it.
explain: «My heart was racing» in a story tells you the heart was beating very fast, not that it was running. Race can be a verb (to race) or a noun (a race). Ask yourself: is this a thing, or an action? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check.
fact: A race is a contest to see who is fastest.
fact: To race means to run or go very fast.
fact: A race can also be a contest to be first to do something, such as the race to the Moon.
fact: «Race» can also mean a large group of people who may share a look or family history. This meaning is less used today, so check with a grown-up if you meet it.
fact: In maths, speed is distance ÷ time, so races help us work out how fast someone goes.
exercise: «Theo lost three races in a row and threw the controller onto the sofa.» How many races did Theo lose? | He never ran in any race | Once, at the very end | He won every race | *Three races in a row
exercise: To race means what? | To shout very loudly | *To run or go very fast | To sleep late in bed | To sit down and read quietly
exercise: «Each squeeze sends a wave along your arteries, and that wave is the pulse you can feel in your wrist after a race in the playground.» Find and copy one word that means the quick beat you can feel in your wrist. | wrist | playground | squeeze | *pulse
test: «My heart was racing» after the playground game. What does this mean? | My heart was in a race | My heart was sad and slow | My heart had stopped beating | *My heart was beating very fast
test: «Changing the target each time someone meets it is moving the goalposts; fair rules are clear before the race starts.» What does this sentence suggest? | Rules should change during the contest | Races have no rules at all | Goalposts are only used in races | *Rules should be clear before a contest begins
exam: In the story «The Tortoise and the Hare», what wins the race? | Fast running by the hare alone | Flying over the finish line | *Slow and steady effort | Sleeping for the whole day
